{
  "core_question": "Should the primary care group accept a Medicare Advantage capitated contract and what deal structure ensures clinical, legal, and financial defensibility?",
  "rationale": "Assessing acceptance requires clinical operations planning to meet quality metrics, legal review of compliance and liability risks, and financial analysis of revenue versus costs and risk mitigation.",
  "routes": ["healthcare", "legal", "finance"],
  "sub_questions": {
    "healthcare": "Given a 40-physician primary care group with a 25,000-member panel offered $1,180 per member per month under a full-risk capitated Medicare Advantage contract that includes HEDIS quality bonuses, what clinical operations strategies and staffing models should be implemented to maintain or exceed required quality metrics while managing high-risk patient populations?",
    "legal": "For the same 40-physician primary care group considering a full-risk capitated Medicare Advantage contract at $1,180 PMPM with HEDIS bonuses, what key legal clauses, compliance safeguards, and risk mitigation measures should be included in the agreement to address CMS regulations, anti-kickback statutes, data privacy, audit requirements, and potential liability for adverse events?",
    "finance": "Assuming a 40-physician primary care group with a 25,000-member panel is offered $1,180 per member per month under a full-risk capitated Medicare Advantage contract that includes HEDIS quality bonuses, how should the group calculate expected revenue, operating costs, risk adjustment adjustments, and reserve requirements to determine financial viability, and what sensitivity analysis should be performed for bonus thresholds and cost fluctuations?"
  },
  "requires_recency_disclosure": true,
  "recency_notes": "CMS Medicare Advantage payment methodology updates, HEDIS bonus thresholds, risk adjustment model changes"
}
